import BlogPostLayout from "../../layouts/BlogPostLayout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
const example = "import csv\nimport os\nimport sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport requests\n\nsource = Path(sys.argv[1])\ndestination = Path(sys.argv[2])\nif source.resolve() == destination.resolve():\n    raise ValueError(\"Choose a different output file\")\n\ndef clean_barcode(value):\n    value = (value or \"\").strip()\n    if not (value.isascii() and value.isdigit()\n            and len(value) in (8, 12, 13, 14)):\n        return None\n    return value\n\nwith source.open(encoding=\"utf-8-sig\", newline=\"\") as handle:\n    reader = csv.DictReader(handle)\n    fields = reader.fieldnames or []\n    if \"barcode\" not in fields:\n        raise ValueError(\"CSV must have a barcode column\")\n    rows = list(reader)\n\nadded = [\"lookup_status\", \"lookup_name\", \"lookup_brand\", \"lookup_image_url\"]\nif set(fields).intersection(added):\n    raise ValueError(\"Input already contains output columns\")\nbarcodes = list(dict.fromkeys(\n    value for row in rows\n    if (value := clean_barcode(row[\"barcode\"])) is not None\n))\nresults = {}\nwith requests.Session() as session:\n    session.headers[\"X-API-Key\"] = os.environ[\"BARCODENEST_API_KEY\"]\n    for offset in range(0, len(barcodes), 100):\n        batch = barcodes[offset:offset + 100]\n        response = session.post(\n            \"https://api.barcodenest.com/v1/products/batch\",\n            json={\"barcodes\": batch},\n            timeout=(5, 60),\n        )\n        response.raise_for_status()\n        items = response.json()[\"results\"]\n        if len(items) != len(batch):\n            raise RuntimeError(\"Unexpected batch response length\")\n        results.update(zip(batch, items))\n\n# Create a new file only after all API requests succeed.\nwith destination.open(\"x\", encoding=\"utf-8\", newline=\"\") as handle:\n    writer = csv.DictWriter(handle, fieldnames=fields + added)\n    writer.writeheader()\n    for row in rows:\n        barcode = clean_barcode(row[\"barcode\"])\n        result = results.get(barcode)\n        product = (result or {}).get(\"product\") or {}\n        status = \"invalid\"\n        if result and result.get(\"valid\"):\n            status = \"found\" if result.get(\"found\") else \"not_found\"\n        writer.writerow({\n            **row,\n            \"lookup_status\": status,\n            \"lookup_name\": product.get(\"name\") or \"\",\n            \"lookup_brand\": product.get(\"brand\") or \"\",\n            \"lookup_image_url\": product.get(\"image_url\") or \"\",\n        })\n";
---
<BlogPostLayout title="Enrich a UPC or EAN Barcode CSV with Product Data in Python | BarcodeNest" heading="How to enrich a CSV of UPC and EAN barcodes with product data" description="Turn a barcode CSV into a product spreadsheet with Python and batch API lookups. Preserve leading zeros, reuse duplicate results, and label missing products." author="BarcodeNest Editorial Team" published="2026-11-19" readingTime="7 min read"><div class="article-shell container"><aside class="article-toc" aria-label="On this page"><strong>On this page</strong><a href="#setup">Set up the integration</a><a href="#script">Run the example</a><a href="#errors">Handle errors</a><a href="#next">Try your own data</a></aside><div class="article-body"><p>A supplier spreadsheet may contain UPC or EAN numbers without the product names, brands and images your catalog needs. You can enrich that CSV by sending the identifiers to a barcode lookup API and writing the returned metadata into new columns. This guide uses Python, the standard CSV module and BarcodeNest's batch endpoint.</p>
<p>The output keeps each original row and adds a lookup status, name, brand and image URL. Duplicate identifiers share one API lookup within the run. You can then review the results before importing them into your inventory or ecommerce system.</p>
<h2 id="prepare">Prepare the barcode CSV</h2>
<p>Create an input file named <code>products.csv</code> with a column called <code>barcode</code>. Additional columns such as your internal SKU or supplier reference will pass through to the output. Keep barcode values as text so leading zeros survive the export.</p>
<CodeBlock label="Example input CSV" language="csv" code={'sku,barcode\nITEM-001,3017620422003\nITEM-002,0036000291452\nITEM-003,3017620422003'} />
<p>Opening a CSV in a spreadsheet can change identifier formatting. If zeros have already been removed, verify the original supplier file before enrichment. Read the <a href="/blog/keep-leading-zeros-barcodes-excel/">Excel leading-zero guide</a> for a safer import workflow.</p>
<h2 id="setup">Set up Python and an API key</h2>
<p>Use Python 3.9 or later and install the HTTP client with <code>python -m pip install requests</code>. <a href="/register/">Create a BarcodeNest account</a>, generate a key, and set the <code>BARCODENEST_API_KEY</code> environment variable in your shell. On PowerShell, use <code>$env:BARCODENEST_API_KEY = "YOUR_API_KEY"</code>. On macOS or Linux, use <code>export BARCODENEST_API_KEY="YOUR_API_KEY"</code>.</p>
<p>The <a href="/docs/barcode-lookup/">batch endpoint</a> accepts up to 100 barcodes per request. Each submitted barcode consumes a lookup unit, so estimate the unique identifiers in your file and check your <a href="/pricing/">plan allowance</a> before running it.</p>
<h2 id="script">Enrich the file in batches</h2>
<p>Save this script as <code>enrich_barcodes.py</code>. It reads a UTF-8 CSV, removes surrounding spaces for lookup, filters unsupported input shapes, and processes unique identifiers in groups of 100. The API checks the barcode check digit.</p>
<CodeBlock label="CSV barcode enrichment" language="python" code={example} />
<p>Run <code>python enrich_barcodes.py products.csv enriched-products.csv</code>. Choose an output filename that does not already exist. Requests finish before the output is created, so an API failure leaves the source file available for another run. The script holds the input and results in memory and suits files that fit within your machine's memory.</p>
<h2 id="review">Review found, missing and invalid rows</h2>
<table><thead><tr><th>lookup_status</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Next step</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>found</td><td>A product record matched.</td><td>Review the name and packaging before import.</td></tr><tr><td>not_found</td><td>The identifier is valid and no record matched.</td><td>Keep the supplier data and queue manual review.</td></tr><tr><td>invalid</td><td>The input shape or check digit failed validation.</td><td>Check the original barcode digits.</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>Optional attributes can be empty even when a product matches. The output preserves the supplier columns and puts API metadata in separate columns so you can decide which values to accept. Returned image URLs are references to images. Downloading, storing or displaying those assets requires checking the applicable source terms.</p>
<h2 id="errors">Handle limits and interrupted jobs</h2>
<p>An authentication error, exhausted allowance, server error or network timeout stops this example. It prints no successful export for an incomplete run. Review the <a href="/docs/errors/">error reference</a> before retrying. A repeat run submits the same identifiers again and may consume additional units. For scheduled imports, persist completed results and a checkpoint between batches so you can resume work.</p>
<p>This example deduplicates exact trimmed strings. UPC-A and its zero-padded GTIN-14 representation may refer to the same identifier while remaining different strings here. Use consistent source formatting or the <a href="/barcode-validator/">barcode validator</a> to inspect canonical GTIN-14 values before planning a larger import.</p>
<h2 id="next">Try your own catalog sample</h2>
<p>Start with a representative file of a few products from the categories you sell. Measure the share of rows that return usable names, brands and images, then review the unmatched rows before scaling up. <a href="/register/">Create an account and test your CSV</a>, or read the <a href="/blog/batch-barcode-lookup-api/">batch lookup guide</a> for the request and response format.</p><p>Prefer uploading a file? <a href="/csv-product-enrichment/">Preview and enrich a CSV in your browser</a>.</p></div></div></BlogPostLayout>
